 or two in each of the last two elections, and its partisan lean now sits about a point to the right of the country. With the term-limited Republican president leaving the ballot open, neither side starts with an incumbency edge, and the state's electoral votes remain genuinely contested.\n\nNo general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet for this race, so the forecast rests on Georgia's fundamentals alone. That points to a margin near even, with a slight Republican tilt. The Democratic field is still unsettled, as Senator Jon Ossoff has repeatedly said he will not run, so candidate choices on both sides could matter more than usual in a state this close.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4567, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4683, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.467, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4627, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4625, "margin": -1.0}]}